# subshell-exit-guards.manifest — the REVIEWED disposition of every site where
# a command word that CAN TERMINATE THE SHELL is evaluated inside a SUBSHELL.
# your-org/nexus-code#1339.
#
# Generated keys:  bash monitor/watcher/subshell-exit-guards.sh
# Locate a row:    bash monitor/watcher/subshell-exit-guards.sh --locate
# Terminators:     bash monitor/watcher/subshell-exit-guards.sh --terminators
# Enforced by:     bash monitor/watcher/test-subshell-exit-guards.sh
#
# Columns (TAB-separated). Fields 1-3 are the KEY; 4-5 are the classifier's
# extraction; 6-7 are the human's verdict:
#   1 file          the shell file the site is in
#   2 line          the site's SOURCE TEXT, whitespace runs collapsed to one
#                   space and the ends trimmed. THIS IS THE KEY, not a comment.
#   3 occurrence    1-based ordinal among sites in the SAME file with the SAME
#                   normalized line, ascending by line number
#   4 word          the command word that can terminate the shell
#   5 fate:chain    `fate` is what happens to the non-zero status; `chain` is
#                   the call path from the word down to a bare `exit`
#   6 disposition   defect | safe | unreviewed
#   7 reason        required for `defect` and `safe`; `unreviewed` needs none
#
# THERE IS NO LINE NUMBER HERE, ON PURPOSE — `#1214`'s measured reason: one
# inserted line elsewhere in a file shifted three recorded sites and the guard
# reported three ADDED and three REMOVED for a tree in which nothing was added
# and nothing removed. A key that is load-bearing on a number every unrelated
# edit moves is not a key. So: file + normalized line + occurrence.
#
# ── THE FATE COLUMN IS THE AXIS, AND WITHOUT IT THIS FILE IS UNREADABLE ──
#
# A subshell `exit` is a DEFECT only when the exit WAS THE GATE. The measured
# instance proves the point inside one file, on one guard:
#
#   name=$(_registry_name_for_workdir …) || name='(unregistered)'   absorbed
#   name=$(service_name_for …)           || exit 1                  propagated
#
# Same construct, same guard, opposite verdicts. So `fate`:
#
#   group       an EXPLICIT `( … )` subshell — the exit IS the group's status
#               and the caller reads it. `( flock -w 10 9 || exit 9; … )`.
#   propagated  `|| exit` — the refusal gates.
#   flow        `|| return`/`continue`/`break` — NOT the same thing.
#               `|| return 0` honours the refusal locally and hands the CALLER
#               a SUCCESS.
#   cond        the substitution is an `if`/`while` CONDITION. NOT automatically
#               safe: on the measured instance `if w=$(_registry_workdir_of …)
#               && [[ … ]]; then die …` short-circuits on the guard's failure,
#               so the die is never reached and the verb falls through.
#   pipe        a pipeline segment; `pipefail` at the site decides.
#   absorbed    `|| <recovery that continues>` — THE MEASURED SHAPE.
#   discarded   no status consumer at all.
#
# `absorbed` and `discarded` are the defect-shaped buckets. `group` and
# `propagated` are dispositioned `safe` in bulk with the reason stated; nothing
# else is, and a row's presence is NOT an accusation — `unreviewed` says
# exactly that.
#
# ── THE COVERAGE BOUNDARY, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THIS FILE ──────────────
#
# `#1339` asks for a DECLARED boundary rather than an implied clean sweep, and
# for the DIRECTION of each error. What the predicate CAN see:
#
#   * `$( )`, backticks, `<( )`, `>( )` and explicit `( )` — nesting-aware,
#     quote-aware, multi-line;
#   * SINGLE-LINE pipelines, every segment (bash without `lastpipe`);
#   * arbitrary INDIRECTION: a least fixpoint over "can terminate the shell",
#     so the measured instance's four-deep chain
#     `_registry_name_for_workdir -> _registry_must_be_readable -> die -> exit`
#     is found from a substituted word that is neither `die` nor `exit`;
#   * terminators DERIVED, never listed — `die` is not assumed to be the only
#     one, and `--terminators` prints what the fixpoint found;
#   * cross-file names resolved through the SOURCE GRAPH (a guard's `die`
#     normally lives in a sourced library).
#
# What it CANNOT see, each with its direction:
#
#   * UNDER-count. A terminator inside a MULTI-LINE compound body that is
#     itself a pipeline segment (`f | while read x; do die …; done`). Modelling
#     it needs balanced if/elif/then/fi and case/)/esac bookkeeping, and
#     getting that wrong LEAKED a frame to end-of-file in 85 of 602 files — a
#     leaked frame is a FALSE finding, which is strictly worse than a missed
#     one for a lint nobody yet has a reason to trust.
#   * UNDER-count. A local definition SHADOWS the source graph, so a name
#     defined locally as a non-terminator hides a sourced terminator of the
#     same name. Correct shell semantics; still an omission.
#   * UNDER-count. `eval`, `$fn` function-name indirection, and a terminator
#     reached only through a `trap` handler are opaque to a static graph.
#   * OVER-count. The source graph is matched by BASENAME, because nearly
#     every `.` in this repo sources through a variable
#     (`. "$_self_dir/../_lib.sh"`), so any corpus file with that basename
#     counts. Wide on purpose: a missed source edge would silently DROP a
#     terminator.
#   * OVER-count, and the largest known one. `fate` is computed from the
#     enclosing statement's `||` arm, so a status captured by `rc=$?` on the
#     following line reads as `discarded`. `slow-band-drift.sh:172`
#     (`out="$(check …)"; local rc=$?`) is such a row and is genuinely safe.
#   * NOT MODELLED AT ALL: `set -e`. Under `set -e` a bare `x=$(f)` whose
#     substitution fails DOES exit, so a `discarded` row in a `set -e` file
#     may gate after all. `monitor/mint-token.sh` is the worked example and
#     its rows say so. Recorded here rather than silently folded into the
#     fate, because `set -e` interacts with `||` lists, functions and
#     conditions in ways a source-text predicate should not pretend to decide.
#
# THE PARSER REFUSES RATHER THAN REPORTING SHORT. Both bugs this classifier
# has had were parser LEAKS producing a large, confident, wrong answer (481
# depth-0 `assert_eq` calls reported as sites the first time; five the second).
# `prepare` and `walk` each assert their own balance as a POST-CONDITION and
# the run exits 3 naming the file it could not parse. A zero from this tool is
# only ever a measured zero.
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS MEANT TO CHANGE. A NEW site is a RED, not a discovery: add
# the row with a disposition and a reason, or fix the site. Turning a
# `defect` row into no row at all is the good outcome.

